Cell["\<\
A stable nonzero gap can describe a scale-consistent geometry with persistent \
anisotropy. A decreasing gap is evidence for this particular Euclidean \
statistic. Even equality of value distributions would not identify which \
directions have which entries. A Euclidean limit requires compatible \
directions across scales and control of both the metric and the shell \
measure. Counting germs instead of shell vertices changes that measure, since \
some endpoints admit more shortest paths.\
\>", "Text",
 CellDingbat->None,ExpressionUUID->"7ee2c1c8-2a91-4ea5-8df7-9324c26aef70"],

Cell[TextData[{
 ButtonBox["The Breathless Line",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/tutorial/\
BreathlessLineTutorial"], None},
  ButtonNote->
   "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/tutorial/BreathlessLineTutorial"],
 " gives a complementary example where normalized path occupation converges \
to a Euclidean segment even though the underlying grid metric remains \
anisotropic."
}], "Text",
